\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Opens -3: Stays Steady; Volume Very Light \b0\par\par\uc2 262 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 1995\par 16:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Around 1545 GMT, Banamex had the spot dollar trading at a mid rate of 7.7650 pesos while chief rival Bancomer quoted the mid rate at 7.7750 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said volume was very light in early trading. \par \par \uc2  'There's a bit of covering of positions, but basically it is very quiet,' said one local trader, adding that he wasn't expecting a lot of movement Friday. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said a lot of market observers thought the exchange rate would trade within a range of 7.70 to 7.80 pesos/dlr Friday, unless a major corporate order surfaces. \par \par \uc2  'There is some end-of year demand, but not a lot, it seems,' the trader said. 'Around 7.80 or 7.85 (pesos/dlr), we could see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  enter.' \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks, most market players seeking to buy dollars have moved in the first 10 minutes of trading, with peso-selling pressures typically easing as the session progressed. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar moved to 7.8150 pesos in the first 30 minutes of trading Thursday before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered some lost ground. Traders on Thursday said many market players felt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar moved much higher. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has confirmed three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Nov. 9, with the most recent one being last Friday, when it entered the market after the spot dollar was flirting with a closing level of 7.80 pesos. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-1