\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's GDP seen rising 3 percent next year. \b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 1995\par 01:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuter) - Mexican finance minister Giuillermo Ortiz said Tuesday he believed the Mexican economy will resume growth in 1996, predicting the country's gross domestic product (GDP) will rise by 3 percent next year. \par \par \uc2  "We believe 1996 will be a year when economic growth will resume," Ortiz told an audience of businessmen, investors and reporters in New York. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz said his GDP forecast was based on expected 15 percent to 16 percent growth of total exports in 1996 and an expected increase in private investments of 5 percent in real terms next year among other things. \par \par \uc2  "Our inflation forecast for 1996 is 20.5 percent," Ortiz said. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a bid to reduce Mexico's peso volatility. Ortiz added, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not defend any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation band for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)...(but) the bank will not be defending any type of band," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drcd00t19\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
