\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -2: Recent Trend Of Stability \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 1995\par 21:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped 0.3% against the dollar last week. It closed 0.7% weaker against the dollar Friday to end the week down 55% against the dollar since Dec. 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed Friday's dip to some foreign institutions seeking dollars in a thin market, but they said there was little foreign participation in Monday's market. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only weakened against its U.S. counterpart in two of the past seven sessions, despite a confirmed uptick in inflation and a bigger-than-expected third-quarter GDP contraction. \par \par \uc2  As reported Thursday, the consumer prices rose 1.38% in the first half of November, compared to 2.06% for all of November. The finance ministry said November 17 that GDP plunged 9.6% in the third-quarter compared to the year-earlier period. Most analysts had been predicting a fall in the neighborhood of 7% to 8%. \par \par \uc2  Some recent sharp gains on the local stock market amid apparent consensus that shares had been oversold has lent some support to the peso. The stock market's key IPC index gained 9.8% last week. An hour before closing Monday, the IPC was down 1.0% to 2,550.61 in light volume amid some profit taking. \par \par \uc2  The peso had been subjected to sharp selling pressures amid light volume in October, when it dropped 9.6% against the dollar to end the month at 7.0650 to the dollar. Those pressures intensified in the first half of November, with the spot dollar closing November 15 at a historic closing high of 7.9100 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped put an end to a further speculative run on the peso in subsequent sessions. As reported, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the market in November, quickly driving down the spot dollar rate after it had soared above 8.00 pesos in intraday trading. Traders say they have seen no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since November 14. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-11-95 \par \par \uc2  2010GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbr09g5y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
