\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Opens -4: Keeps Trading At 7.6550/Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 1995\par 17:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Shortly before 1600 GMT, the spot dollar continued to be quoted at a mid rate of 7.6550 pesos in very light volume. \par \par \uc2  'There's a bit of (Mexican) corporate demand for dollars, but there's also some offering of dollars due to the recent (peso) stability,' said another local trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a lot of Mexican companies already appeared to have covered their dollar needs in earlier weeks, which may ease typical month's end dollar pressures. They added that foreign participation in the market was lighter in early trading than it was on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The discovery late last week of an $84-million Swiss bank account held under a false name by Raul Salinas, brother of former President Carlos Salinas, continued to be the topic of discussion on the local trading floor. The news has sparked renewed local media speculation on the extent of corruption under the previous administration, but dealers said it was not adversely affecting the market. Carlos Salinas issued a statement on the weekend saying he had not been aware of any financial wrongdoing on the part of his brother, who had held various government posts. \par \par \uc2  A recent drop in interest rates has accompanied the recent the stabilization of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bellwether 28-day Cetes rates reversed eight weeks of rises to fall 5.25 percentage points to 54.74% at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly Treasury bill auction last Tuesday, and secondary money market rates fell for a seventh straight session Friday. Primary rates could drop further this Tuesday, according to market observers. \par \par \uc2  The peso had been subjected to sharp selling pressures amid light volume in October, when it dropped 9.6% against the dollar to end the month at 7.0650 to the dollar. Those pressures intensified in the first half of November, with the spot dollar closing November 15 at a historic closing high of 7.9100 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped put an end to a further speculative run on the peso in subsequent sessions. As reported, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the market in November, quickly driving down the spot dollar rate after it had soared above 8.00 pesos in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1600GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbr09f0k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
