\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -3: Economic Targets Still Questioned \b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1995\par 21:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Market observers attributed Thursday's rally in part to unsubstantiated rumors that some G-7 countries' central banks were preparing to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  support to the peso. The spot dollar dipped as low as 7.7000 pesos in the first hour of trading Thursday, before the market discounted the rumor. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official reiterated Friday that the Bank of Mexico had no information on any international peso support plan. The fact that the market took the rumor and ran with it, though, appeared to signal a shift in sentiment after several weeks of bearish rumors, according to observers. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry reiterated this week that it remains committed to a free-floating exchange rate regime, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stressed that it reserves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in a discretionary manner. \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves stood at $14.063 billion as of November 10. \par \par \uc2  The recent peso stability comes despite some mildly negative reaction to the government's 1996 proposed budget presented to Congress Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the government maintained its commitment to a balanced budget, a lot of analysts and investors have questioned some of the blueprint's macro-economic assumptions, which include 3.0% growth and 20.5% inflation for next year. Those projections are unchanged from targets first outlined October 29 in the most recent multi-sector wage-price pact. Recent market volatility had some analysts expecting revisions to growth and inflation numbers. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry said Tuesday it expected the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to average 6.3100 pesos in 1995 and 7.7000 pesos in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Some observers, though, also think those estimates may be too optimistic. An economist with the Nomura Research Institute predicted Wednesday that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely end 1996 at 9.00 pesos per dollar, while an analyst with Goldman Sachs projected the exchange rate moving to 9.50 pesos/dlr next year. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-11-95 \par \par \uc2  2036GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbh094pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
