\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's Peso Opens -4:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Factor Cited \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1995\par 17:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Around 1605 GMT, Banamex quoted the spot dollar trading at a mid rate of 7.7000 pesos on a spread of 7.6500/7.7500. Banco Inverlat had the spot dollar mid rate higher at 7.7350 pesos on a narrower spread of 7.7100/7.7600. \par \par \uc2  The peso rebounded from three straight sessions of declines to close 2.2% stronger against the dollar Thursday. \par \par \uc2  As reported, the spot dollar reached a new historic closing high of 7.9100 pesos Wednesday, when the peso dropped 1.2%. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended Thursday down 55% against the dollar since Dec. 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been subjected to heightened selling pressures in the last six weeks. The spot dollar ended September at 6.3850 pesos, and then climbed higher in October to close the month at 7.0650 pesos before a new round of peso-selling began in November. \par \par \uc2  Market observers attributed Thursday's rally in part to unsubstantiated rumors that some G-7 countries' central banks were preparing to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  support to the peso. The spot dollar dipped as low as 7.7000 pesos in the first hour of trading Thursday, before the market discounted the rumor. \par \par \uc2  Traders said market players have treaded cautiously in recent days amid the threat of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As reported, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin trading November 9 to push the spot dollar down to 7.5500 pesos after it had soared as high as 8.2500 pesos in earlier trading. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid light volume Tuesday, pushing the spot dollar down to 7.8100 pesos after it had again moved above 8.0000 pesos in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  'Volume is very light today, and there seems to be general consensus that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again around eight (pesos to the dollar),' said a local trader. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry reiterated this week that it remains committed to a free-floating exchange rate regime, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stressed that it reserves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in a discretionary manner. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1609GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbh094hz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
