\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's Peso Closes -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Discounted \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 1995\par 21:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  According to Banamex figures, the peso closed 2.2% stronger against the dollar from Wednesday's close, and is down 55% against the dollar since Dec. 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said volume was moderate Thursday, with swaps among Mexican banks dominating the action. There were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders said market players continued to play it cautious. \par \par \uc2  'Volume is still not all that great, and a lot of people think the Banco de Mexico would not hesitate to move in again if the peso moves back above 8.00 (to the dollar),' said a local trader. \par \par \uc2  A spokeswoman with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last Thursday and again on Tuesday after the spot dollar soared above 8.00 pesos. The spot dollar hit a historic intraday high of 8.2500 pesos last Thursday before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Thursday peso rebound came despite the fact that 48-hour spot contracts will be tied up until next Tuesday. Mexican financial markets will be shut Monday for a national holiday. In recent months investors have shied away from contracting pesos ahead of three-day weekends. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the finance ministry said late Wednesday that the public sector registered a surplus of 17.40 billion pesos for the first nine months of 1995, a nominal 64% increase from 1994. It also said Mexico's domestic public debt as of the end of September had dropped 14 billion pesos from the end of the second quarter, principally through amortizations of dollar-linked Tesobonos. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 16-11-95 \par \par \uc2  2025GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbg091cj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
