\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Opens -3: Strengthens To 7.8200/Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 1995\par 16:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A little before 1545 GMT, the spot dollar moved lower to 7.8200 pesos on a spread of 7.7700/7.8700 in what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers described as moderate volume. \par \par \uc2  'I don't know who's buying, but there don't appear to be any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' said a local trader with a U.S. bank. 'The drop in secondary (money market) rates is being taken as a good sign.' \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last Thursday and again on Tuesday after the spot dollar soared above 8.00 pesos. The spot dollar hit a historic intraday high of 8.2500 pesos last Thursday before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A trader with a Mexican financial institution said he saw some foreign investors seeking dollars. \par \par \uc2  'This looks like a pretty natural rally,' the local trader said. \par \par \uc2  The early peso rebound comes despite the fact that 48-hour spot contracts will be tied up until next Tuesday. Mexican financial markets will be shut Monday for a national holiday. In recent months investors have shied away from contracting pesos ahead of three-day weekends. \par \par \uc2  The rally also comes despite some mildly negative reaction to the government's 1996 proposed budget presented to Congress Tuesday. Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz will defend the budget proposal in a give-and-take session with Congress Friday. \par \par \uc2  Although the government maintained its commitment to a balanced budget, a lot of analysts and investors have questioned some of the blueprint's macro-economic assumptions, which include 3.0% growth and 20.5% inflatin for next year. Those projections are unchanged from targets first outlined October 29 in the most recent multi-sector wage-price pact. The peso, though, closed Wednesday down 11% from the end of October, when it ended at 7.0650 to the dollar. Despite Wednesday's dip in secondary money market rates, interest rates also have climbed higher since the end of October. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 16-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1547GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbg090g3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
