\par\par \b \uc2  D'Amato Again Calls For Release Of IMF Report On Mexico \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 1995\par 21:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON (AP-DJ)--Senate Banking Committee Chairman Alfonse D'Amato called again Wednesday for the Treasury Department to release a classified report on Mexico that was drafted earlier this year by the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  D'Amato, a New York Republican, is demanding Treasury declassify the report covering an internal IMF probe of the circumstances surrounding its surveillance of Mexico in the runup to its December 1994 financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The crisis prompted the Clinton administration to launch a $20-billion bailout effort, which has sparked a backlash on Capitol Hill, prominently from Republicans, including D'Amato. \par \par \uc2  The senator, speaking on the floor of the Senate, maintained Wednesday that disclosure is increasingly important since \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators are now capitalizing on recent peso \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  D'Amato maintained that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is essentially being financed through the $11.8 billion in outstanding U.S. credit to Mexico. \par \par \uc2  General details about the IMF report has been dribbling out, although D'Amato is demanding full disclosure of the document that Treasury classified citing national security concerns. \par \par \uc2  D'Amato has received a copy of the classified report, he confirmed. But he added that he hasn't read the report 'because I'll probably come down to the floor of the Senate and expose the bungling' by the IMF and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  As reported, the internal probe found a range of shortcomings involving IMF staff, the fund's 24-member executive board and the Mexican government into last December's crisis. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus has also maintained that the report's findings weren't surprising and that management had implemented corrective actions. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-11-95 \par \par \uc2  2009GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbf090jg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
