\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervenes \highlight\uc2  for second time to bolster peso. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Henry Tricks \par \uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 1995\par 00:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second time to shore up the stricken peso after investors ditched the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in disappointment at a long-awaited budget proposal for 1996. \par \par \uc2  A Banco de Mexico official confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold a "small amount" of dollars to support the Mexican peso, as it did last Thursday when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an all-time low. \par \par \uc2  From a level of around 8.20 per dollar in the peso's closely-watched 48-hour contract, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to end the session at 7.79, a three-centavo gain over Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  That was just token respite in a week in which the peso has been hammered by investor fears that the government has lost its way on economic policy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also were asking how much longer the government could afford to go on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to back the peso, given its relatively scanty reserves, the bulk of which have been borrowed from the United States. \par \par \uc2  Peso worries resurfaced Tuesday after the government unveiled a budget proposal for 1996 that analysts said contained unrealistic growth and inflation projections. It also fell short of what they had hoped would be a bold programme to resuscitate the country's crisis-torn economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minster Guillermo Ortiz paid no attention to recommendations for a U-turn on economic policy. He said he would stand his ground and not be bossed around by speculators. \par \par \uc2  "I want to emphasise that speculators will not make us change course. That would be equivalent to playing their game, validating the climate of uncertainty that they have sown in recent days," he told Congress. \par \par \uc2  The proposal for a balanced budget was drawn up on assumptions that Mexico's economy would grow 3 percent next year, with an inflation rate of 20.5 percent, Ortiz said. \par \par \uc2  Underpinning that is a projected average exchange rate of 7.7 pesos per dollar, below Tuesday's level and one that many analysts consider wildly optimisitic. \par \par \uc2  "It makes you wonder what country they're living in," one stock trader said. \par \par \uc2  Gray Newman, economist at James Capel in Mexico City, welcomed the government's commitment to what he called responsible fiscal and monetary policy but he said it may fall on deaf ears. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not sure this will help calm the markets because concerns over fiscal policy are not driving the market right now. It's more of a credibility issue," he said. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates have more than doubled to above 70 percent in the last two months as the government battles a speculative attack on the peso by offering almost irresitible returns. \par \par \uc2  The immediate drawback is that Mexican banks are highly vulnerable to the higher rates. In the longer-term, such cripplingly tight monetary policy undermines the very rebound from recession the government says it is aiming to achieve. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drbe01uae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
