\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -4: Weakens Further To 8.1500 Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 1995\par 16:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A little after 1555 GMT, the spot dollar was being quoted at a mid rate of 8.1500 pesos on a mid rate of 8.1000/8.2000. \par \par \uc2  A Mexico City-based trader with a U.S. bank said he saw demand for dollars both among Mexican nationals and foreigners. \par \par \uc2  In outlining the most recent multi-sector wage-price agreement of October 29, the government said it expected 3% GDP growth and 20% inflation for next year. Analysts and market players have said those figures may now be too optimistic, especially in light of recent peso plunges and interest rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  'People want to see if and how the government will change its targets,' said one local trader. 'People are looking for some more definition.' \par \par \uc2  Bear Stearns in New York is estimating 45% inflation for 1996 - and that estimate is based on a projected year-end exchange rate of 6.7500 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2  David Malpass, an economist with Bear Stearns, said Monday whether the peso can retrace its lost ground 'depends completely on \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  answering the purpose behind high interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' \par \par \uc2  A lot of analysts say at this point it is unclear where the Mexican government wants the peso to trade against the dollar. Speculation has persisted for months that the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have differed on Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  The peso fell 9.6% against the dollar in October to end the month at 7.0650 pesos, and has taken further hard hits in the first two weeks of November. \par \par \uc2  It ended Monday down 55% against the dollar since December 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1558GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbe08yiw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
