\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico investors dig in, see slim hope of US help. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Caroline Brothers \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1995\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 10 (Reuter) - Foreign investors in Mexico are holding steady despite the peso's slide analysts said on Friday, seeing little hope of U.S. help this time round but drawing some cheer from a rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The United States and the IMF were forced to bail Mexico out a year ago when a bungled peso devaluation saw foreign capital flee emerging markets world-wide. \par \par \uc2  But news that Washington was facing the prospect of a debt default by its own federal government dashed what little hope there was of the U.S. coming to Mexico's rescue a second time. \par \par \uc2  "The idea of using U.S. tax payers' money to support the Mexican peso is simply not on the cards," said Peter West, economic advisor at West Merchant Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico is not particularly popular in the U.S. at the moment," added Rupert White, head of Latin American equity sales at James Capel in London. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors say the peso's plunge to an all-time low on Thursday was driven by a speculative attack on the foreign exchanges and was not justified by Mexico's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican government has done all in its power to set the ecomomy straight. It is running a trade surplus, credit and inflation are under control, it agreed a pact with labour and unions to encourage modest growth and it has even paid back some of its U.S. borrowings. \par \par \uc2  Fund managers and analysts alike said the overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso was well-timed and would deter fresh assaults in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "They achieved quite a lot by spending very little," West said. "Quite a lot of people got their fingers caught in the door." \par \par \uc2  Despite limited foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Mexico spent an estimated $150 million to $200 million in a rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lifting the peso from its post-war low of 8.30 against the dollar to a more respectable 7.60. \par \par \uc2  But Mexico-watchers warned that the low level of Mexico's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves would not permit unlimited support. \par \par \uc2  Mexico has borrowed some $12 billion out of $20 billion available in U.S. credit facilities agreed by U.S. President Bill Clinton earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  But this is earmarked for paying off debt and Mexico would have an uphill battle renegotiating the terms of the borrowings for permission to use them as a peso war chest. \par \par \uc2  Capel's White said this was not impossible, though the likelihood was small. \par \par \uc2  With the peso weakness weighing on U.S. dollar sentiment, the United States cannot remain unconcerned by what is happening to its neighbour. \par \par \uc2  But with Clinton proposing to shut down the federal government next week amid an increasingly likelihood of default on federal debt, Mexico is not high on the U.S. agenda. \par \par \uc2  And last night longtime critic of U.S. aid to Mexico, Republican Senator Alfonse d'Amato, voiced widespread opposition in Congress. \par \par \uc2  "The Mexican government will spend U.S. taxpayers dollars in a frantic and futile attempt to support the peso," he said. \par \par \uc2  While most foreign funds are not greatly exposed to Mexico, unlike this time last year when many got burned in the "Tequila crisis", some did get tempted back in a rally last March and are taking a big loss now. \par \par \uc2  "Cumulatively though people are underweight in Mexico at the moment," White said, seeing a limit to the impact of this round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  troubles. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drba02531\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
