\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Flat At 7.5500 VS Dlr; Few Trades \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1995\par 20:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso recovered from early declines to close even against the dollar in very light volume Friday, one day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to put the brakes on a pronounced peso sell-off. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex and a few other banks quoted the dollar ending at mid rate levels of 7.5500 pesos, even with Thursday's close. Spreads remained 7.5000/7.6000 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2  A couple other banks had the spot dollar ending higher at 7.6000 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said volume was extremely low, with market players treading cautiously after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the closing minutes of trading Thursday. \par \par \uc2  'Everybody figures the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very close to the market, and that it will move quickly if there's any big shift,' said one local trader with a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  Volume was so light, according to dealers, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably could have pushed the spot dollar to 7.0000 pesos with as little as $100 million. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar moved as high as 7.7700 pesos in the first 90 minutes of trading before it dropped back below 7.7000 pesos shortly afterward. \par \par \uc2  'We saw some demand for dollars again today,' said another local dealer with a Mexican bank, adding that most buy orders appeared to come in early trading from Mexican corporations looking to take advantage of the peso's 3.3% closing rally Thursday. \par \par \uc2  More than a few traders speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have injected some dollars into the market again Friday, although in much smaller amounts. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official dismissed the speculation. \par \par \uc2  'Up until 12 (1800 GMT) there has been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  whatsoever,' the official said, adding that the bank reserved the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market 'in a discretionary manner.' \par \par \uc2  As reported, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the final minutes of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Thursday to halt a major sell-off of pesos that had pushed the spot dollar to a historic intraday high of 8.2500 pesos. The spot dollar was trading around 8.0000 pesos when the sudden \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred. \par \par \uc2  The peso dived 4.0% against the dollar in very light volume Wednesday to end at a historic closing low of 7.8000 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1959GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drba08x18\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
