\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -2: Rumors, Nervousness Dominate Mkt \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 1995\par 21:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Traders said volume was extremely heavy in late morning trading, as U.S. financial institutions dumped their pesos and Mexican banks scrambled to take long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  'Everybody was freaking out, with a lot of rumors,' said one peso dealer in New York. 'There was a lot of panic selling.' \par \par \uc2  Market nervousness eased in the final hour of trading, though, as the peso recovered some lost ground. \par \par \uc2  'The last 60 minutes or so were very quiet,' said one local trader. 'We saw some real spurts of activity, but overall volume was moderate.' \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar opened sharply higher at 7.2050 pesos before trading settled down for about 90 minutes. It then surged to a mid rate of 7.4450 pesos at midday before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged a mini-rally. \par \par \uc2  'There was a strong rumor of a major American investment fund buying dollars, and that helped prompt a second run (against the peso),' said another local trader. \par \par \uc2  A smaller rumor that Mexico had already spent some 1 billion German marks of an international debt issue placed in early October also circulated on trading floors. A finance ministry spokesman said later that Mexico will receive the funds Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar liquidity remained a serious problem, with no foreign funds interested in entering Mexico. \par \par \uc2  'Nobody is coming in, so Mexicans who want to get out will sell at any rate,' said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  did issue 1.77 billion pesos in 78-day credits at 52.18% in the secondary money market in late trading. It had issued credits at a little under 46% Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-11-95 \par \par \uc2  2041GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drb108kn0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
