\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Weaker At 7.2350/Dlr:New 7-Mth Low \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 1995\par 22:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso tumbled against the dollar Thursday to close at its weakest level in more than seven months. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex and another major bank, Inverlat, had the dollar ending at mid rate levels of 7.2350 pesos against 6.7850 pesos at Wednesday's close. Spreads were 7.2100/7.2600 pesos per dollar, compared with 6.7750/6.7950 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Other banks had the peso ending even weaker. Bancomer, the country's second-biggest bank, had the spot dollar ending at a mid rate of 7.2650 pesos. Banca Cremi and Bancrecer, two smaller banks, pegged the spot dollar at a closing mid rate of 7.2850 pesos, underlining the day's speculative volatility. \par \par \uc2  It was the most pronounced peso sell-off since March 9, when the spot dollar soared to an intraday high of 7.7000 pesos before finally settling at 7.4500 pesos - the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakest closing level since the government was forced to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  December 20 and adopt a free floating exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed Thursday's sharp peso decline to heightened nervousness on both the political and the economic fronts. \par \par \uc2  The local currrency lost ground from the opening bell amid mounting concern about the cease-fire southern state of Chiapas, where Zapatista rebels said late Wednesday they had gone on heightened alert after the weekend arrest of a top rebel leader in Mexico City. Some rumors swept the market early on that federal troops and rebels were preparing to clash for the first time since January 1994. There have been no reported clashes. \par \par \uc2  Reports early Thursday that Russian President Boris Yeltsin had been rushed to hospital with an apparent heart attack also prompted a sharp exodus of U.S. investors as they converted back into dollars, traditionally seen as a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven in times of international uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  'The market was nervous to begin with, and Chiapas and Yeltsin were enough to cause panic,' said one local trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said volume picked up considerably from recent days, although it remained moderate in absolute terms. Foreigners have kept their distance from the Mexican market all month, causing a prolonged liquidity shortage. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar climbed above 7.30 pesos in late intraday trading before the peso fought back some last ground in the final thirty minutes of trading. \par \par \uc2  There were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  did, however, help push secondary money market rates sharply higher Thursday in an attempt to lend some support to the peso. \par \par \uc2  In early afternoon trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued 1.17 billion pesos in 26-day credits at 54.46%. Overnight repurchase agreements on government paper, meanwhile, rose to 52.0% in intraday trading. As reported, Cetes repurchase agreements averaged 41.04% Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 26-10-95 \par \par \uc2  2011GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025draq08g7e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
