\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Sharply Weaker At 6.7450 Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 1995\par 21:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso closed sharply weaker against the dollar for a second straight session Tuesday to move to a new six-month low as foreign investors kept their distance and Mexican market players continued to fret over the domestic economy and political climate. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was fairly strong consensus that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market indirectly, putting the brakes on even bigger peso losses. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on spot dollar markets, the dollar ended at mid rate levels of 6.7450 pesos on a spread of 6.7300/6.7600 pesos per dollar. Closing spot dollar mid rate quotations Monday ranged from 6.6400 pesos to 6.6750 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar last closed higher April 3, when it ended at 6.7650 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar moved to a mid rate of 6.8300 pesos in the first hour of trading Monday before the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded in later trading. According to dealers, there was wide agreement that the Bank of Mexico sold dollars through leading Mexican financial intermediaries to halt a further slide. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not comment on rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'There was some panic selling going on among Mexicans in early trading, and most people are pretty certain the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved in to stabilize things a bit,' said one local trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said volume was moderate, with very little foreign participation. That shortage of dollar liquidity pushed the exchange rate up as nervous local investors scrambled to sell their pesos. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar ended Friday at 6.5400 pesos and closed September at 6.3850 pesos. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-10-95 \par \par \uc2  1957GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025draa07y58\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
