\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Weaker Vs Dlr; U.S. House Cited \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 1995\par 21:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso closed weaker against the dollar for the third straight session in light to moderate volume Thursday following news of a possible cut-off of U.S. funding to Mexico. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on spot dollar markets, the dollar ended at mid rate levels of 6.1350 pesos against 6.1000 pesos at Wednesday's close. Spreads were 6.1200/6.1500 pesos per dollar, compared with 6.0900/6.1100 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso took an early hit after the U.S. House late Wednesday approved a measure aimed at stopping future use of the U.S. Treasury's Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) to bolster foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If it becomes law, the ammendment could cut off further U.S. financial aid to Mexico beginning in October. Some $7.5 billion of a $20 billion U.S. credit package for Mexico that remains outstanding would be affected by the measure. \par \par \uc2  But after the spot dollar flirted with a level of 6.2000 pesos amid an active peso sell-off in the first hour of trading, pressures against the peso eased as volume also fell off. \par \par \uc2  'There was some definite nervousness early on, but it never moved toward panic,' said one Mexico City trader. \par \par \uc2  'The market is so delicate right now that any news will move it,' added another trader. \par \par \uc2  There was some speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have made a mild \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm the market early on, but most traders said the market corrected itself. \par \par \uc2  A lot of Wednesday's volume consisted of speculative moves by Mexican banks, including the covering of short positions. Foreigner market players for the most part stayed away. \par \par \uc2  A drop in foreign participation in the local stock market also worked against the peso Thursday, traders added. Around 1945 GMT, the stock exchange's key IPC index was up 0.3% at 2,443.43, after having traded below Wednesday's close for most of the day. The IPC had fallen in the previous four sessions as foreign portfolios took some profits after having helped run the index up 15% in the first two weeks of July. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 20-07-95 \par \par \uc2  1950GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr7k05k91\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
