\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -2: Still Far Above Govt's Forecast \b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1995\par 21:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The new peso closed 0.3% stronger against the dollar from Friday's close, and is down 49% against the dollar since Dec. 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  An encouraging February trade surplus and a tightening of the money supply in the absence of any major social unrest all have contributed to the recent rebound of the peso from its March 22 closing rate of 7.1000 pesos/dlr, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that improvement in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come without the help of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the first months of the crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was frequently reported by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers. \par \par \uc2  But despite the recent gains and heightened stability, the peso is still trading above its 6.3000 pesos/dlr closing level of March 10, when the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared against the dollar on the strength of a tough government austerity program unveiled the night before. \par \par \uc2  It is also nowhere near the government's preferred 1995 trading level of 6 pesos to the dollar. That preferred level was announced March 9, after the administration of President Ernesto Zedillo conceded its earlier preferred range of 4.5 pesos to the dollar was unfeasible. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 03-04-95 \par \par \uc2  1953GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4302c1n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\