\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Weaker For Third Straight Day \b0\par\par\uc2 243 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 21:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso closed weaker against the dollar Wednesday for the third straight day following an 18% rebound Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on spot and 24-hour dollar markets, the dollar ended at mid rate levels of 6.9500 pesos per dollar against 6.6350 pesos per dollar at Tuesday's close. Spreads were 6.9000/7.0000 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an intraday high of 7.3500 around 1830 GMT before rebounding in the final hour of trading. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring about the late rebound, perhaps with as much as $100 million. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not confirm any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that volume was light to moderate, and that speculation and pessimism continued to be directing many of the transactions in the wake of weeks of market volatility. \par \par \uc2  'Investors are still uneasy,' said one Mexico City trader. \par \par \uc2  'Nobody believes the government anymore,' added another dealer. \par \par \uc2  Traders said more uneasiness was created Wednesday because peso demand was still minimal despite a rise of 24.39 percentage points in 27-day Cetes, or Treasury bills, to yields of 82.38% at Wednesday's primary auction. \par \par \uc2  Although demand outstripped offerings, one dealer said most of the bids came from domestic investors as many foreign investors remained on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-03-95 \par \par \uc2  2000GMT