\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Closes Weaker For Second Straight Day \b0\par\par\uc2 290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 21:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's peso closed weaker against the dollar Tuesday for the second straight trading day after an 18% rebound last Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, on the spot dollar market, the dollar ended at mid rate levels of 6.6350 pesos per dollar against 6.4750 pesos per dollar at Monday's close. Spreads were 6.6100/6.6600 pesos per dollar, compared to spreads of 6.4500/6.5000 pesos/dlr at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said downward pressure was created on the peso as holders of maturing dollar-linked government paper known as Tesobonos attempted to increase their yields. A little more than $500 million dollars in Tesobonos mature this Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to keep the peso from falling too much ahead of Thursday's amortization. There was no confirmation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz conceded during a Tuesday briefing to financial investors and reporters in New York that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  'will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time' in a bid to lend stability to the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The peso recovered 18% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday to close at 6.3000 pesos/dlr after the government unveiled a tough emergency economic plan that calls for a 50% sales tax hike, double-digit increases in gasoline, gas and electricity, and a tightening of the fiscal belt. \par \par \uc2  But the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped 2.7% against the dollar Monday in light, short-term profit-taking and investor concern that soaring interest rates could cause a stronger contraction in the economy than originally anticipated, traders said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-03-95 \par \par \uc2  2003GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr3e01vks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
